A||N||7||LPR||Over Heating?||||5||
Z||000000||LPR||03-21-2001||05:19 PM||||I may have two separate problems, but together they make one fatal one.  First my Temp. Gage doesn’t work.  It spikes when I turn the key, but then reads that the engine is cold all the time.<P>The other related problem is the fact that I had to replace the original engine because of severe overheating killed the compression.  I installed a new engine but haven’t noticed the fan come on to cool either engine.  I don’t want my new engine to die like the first!  I can’t tell if it is overheating or not because the gage doesn’t work! I don’t have AC either to I can’t test the switch!<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||862M4inCA||03-21-2001||05:44 PM||||well, you can hook up a mechanical temp gauge to get a temp reading. Also, to determine if the fan is working there is a switch on the engine that grounds out at 235 degrees and turns the fan relay on. This switch is located on the back left hand side of the head on the 4 cyl. Just ground that plug and see if the fan is coming on, if not it could either be a relay or the fan itself. Also, if you have access to a scanner you can pull the temp from the ECM since it also has it's own temp sensor on the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000002||862M4inCA||03-21-2001||05:44 PM||||well, you can hook up a mechanical temp gauge to get a temp reading. Also, to determine if the fan is working there is a switch on the engine that grounds out at 235 degrees and turns the fan relay on. This switch is located on the back left hand side of the head on the 4 cyl. Just ground that plug and see if the fan is coming on, if not it could either be a relay or the fan itself. Also, if you have access to a scanner you can pull the temp from the ECM since it also has it's own temp sensor on the engine.||||reg||1
